  "output": "The incident under review manifested as an abrupt escalation of queue depth between 09:00 and 09:10, followed by a cascade of latency spikes, elevated retry volume, and limited mitigation from additional workers. The synthetic evidence points to a single dominant driver: dependency B began throttling requests, returning HTTP 429 responses for roughly one‑third of outbound calls at 09:15 (E05). This rate‑limit event triggered a surge in retry attempts (E06), which compounded the backlog by re‑inserting failed work into the queue, inflating both queue depth and task latency (E03). The retry amplification was further evidenced by the dramatic reduction in queue depth when retries were temporarily disabled (E08), indicating that the retry loop itself was a key lever of pressure on the system. Adding workers from eight to sixteen produced only a modest 6 % reduction in queue depth over ten minutes (E07), confirming that the bottleneck lay external to the processing capacity; the workers could not consume work faster than it was being regenerated by the retry mechanism.\n\nThe analysis distinguishes primary cause and amplifiers as follows:\n- Primary cause: Dependency B’s enforced rate limit (E05) that shifted a substantial portion of requests into the error path.\n- Amplifiers: The exponential backoff retry policy (E06) that multiplied the effective request rate toward the dependency, and the static worker pool size that lacked elasticity to absorb the extra load (E07).\n\nThe retry policy, while protecting downstream services from immediate overload, proved counter‑productive in this scenario. The three‑retry ceiling combined with exponential backoff generated a 420 % increase in retry volume (E06). This surge re‑queued work faster than the workers could process it, forming a feedback loop that deepened the queue. When the policy was relaxed—by disabling retries—the queue depth fell 38 % within six minutes (E08), albeit at the cost of a higher direct failure rate, demonstrating that the policy’s aggressiveness was the principal amplifier.\n\nAdding workers had limited effect because the root bottleneck was not CPU, memory, or disk saturation (E04) but the external dependency’s throttling. Even with double the workers, the system could only process work at its intrinsic rate; the majority of work was being re‑queued by the retry logic before completion, so additional compute resources could not translate into proportional throughput gains.\n\nUncertainties remain around the exact latency contribution of network jitter, the potential impact of hidden internal service throttles, and whether other downstream services experienced similar rate limits. The evidence set does not capture granular per‑request timing or internal queue‑scheduling logs, leaving a margin for alternative interpretations.\n\nA staged remediation plan is therefore recommended. Stage 1 addresses the immediate symptom by adjusting the retry policy: reduce maximum retries, introduce jitter, and back‑off more aggressively to curb re‑queueing. Stage 2 coordinates with the owners of dependency B to raise rate‑limit thresholds or implement a circuit‑breaker that fails fast without triggering retries. Stage 3 expands worker elasticity through auto‑scaling based on queue depth, ensuring that capacity can respond dynamically if future spikes occur. Stage 4 adds observability—enhanced metrics on retry counts, dependency response codes, and per‑task latency—to reduce uncertainty and provide early warning of similar incidents.\n\nAn alternative hypothesis considered was an internal CPU or memory contention causing processing slowdown. However, CPU utilization remained moderate (43 %) and memory usage stable (51 %) at the time of the incident (E04), and no deploy, schema change, host restart, or network alteration occurred (E10). These conditions make an internal resource saturation scenario unlikely compared with the clear external throttling signal from dependency B.\n\nIn conclusion, the queue incident was primarily driven by dependency B’s rate limiting, amplified by an overly aggressive retry strategy and a static worker pool. Adjusting retries, collaborating with the dependency owner, and improving scaling and visibility constitute a comprehensive remediation pathway.",
